%[text] %[text:anchor:T_2859B247] # Fun with Raw Images
%[text] A raw image is the digital negative captured by most high-end cameras (and now even some smart phones). In this module, we will explore a raw image captured by one of the program's cameras - a Sony SLT-A77V. (Strictly, it is an SLT, not an SLR: instead of a mirror that flips up out of the way, it uses a fixed translucent mirror that lets most of the light through to the sensor.)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%[text] In this script, we will open a RAW file and process it ourselves, one step at a time.
%[text] Remember, the color sensors in a camera have a Bayer Filter on them:
%[text] ![](text:image:247d)![](text:image:2f76)
%[text] We will demosaic the image and correct the contrast and white balance. Finally, we will compare the RAW result to the demosaiced file.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_871AE746] ## Open image
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_92F99E8E] In the Unit 2 data folder, I have added a file called 'vertebra.dng'. The DNG extension indicates that this file is a raw file. Notice the rather large size of the file (about 51 MB) - we will come back to why it is worth carrying that much data around.
%[text] Since it is a raw file, we are going to start by using a special tiff reader. I have packaged this reader in the course function **`mmReadRAWimage`**.
clc
clearvars
mmSetUnitDataFolder(2)
[rgb, meta] = mmReadRAWimage("vertebra.dng");
figure('Name','RAW')
imshow(rgb)
size(rgb)
%[text] - Notice that the image returned is actually an RGB image, not the RAW image that we're after.
%[text] - This is the reason why the image file is so large. It contains both a raw and an RGB version of the image.
%[text] - Also, notice that the RGB image has a strong yellow cast. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F364DB65] ## Inspect the Metadata
%[text] Read the metadata
meta
%[text] - Hmm, it says it's a true color image (and 24-bit)
%[text] - And that it's only 171x256
%[text] - This is just the preview image \
%%
%[text] - This image was taken on one of our cameras \
meta.Make
meta.Model
%%
%[text] We have to dig down deeper in the metadata to find the raw image. Notice these SubIFDs:
meta.SubIFDs{1} % the meta data for the raw image
%[text] Ok, notice
%[text] -  BitDepth = 16
%[text] - Colortype = 'CFA' - color filter array (aka Bayer Filter) \
%[text] This looks like it.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EXIF] ### How was this photo actually taken?
%[text] A raw file is not just pixels - it is a complete record of the exposure. The function **`rawinfo`** pulls that record out for us, and the `ExifTags` field holds the settings the photographer dialed in.
rawInfo = rawinfo('vertebra.dng'); % modern raw metadata reader
exif = rawInfo.ExifTags
%[text] There is your **exposure triangle**, recorded at the moment the shutter fired:
s = sprintf('Exposure Triangle:\n');
s = [s sprintf('Shutter : 1/%.0f s\n', 1/exif.ExposureTime)];
s = [s sprintf('Aperture: f/%.1f\n', exif.FNumber)];
s = [s sprintf('ISO     : %d\n', exif.ISOSpeedRatings)];
s = [s sprintf('Focal   : %d mm (%d mm equivalent on 35mm)\n', exif.FocalLength, exif.FocalLengthIn35mmFilm)];
fprintf('%sMetering: %s, light source: %s\n', s, exif.MeteringMode, exif.LightSource);
%[text] A few things worth discussing before we touch a single pixel:
%[text] - The lens was at 75 mm, which on this camera's sensor frames like a **112 mm** lens would on 35mm film.
%[text] - The old **reciprocal rule** says that to hand-hold a 112 mm equivalent without shake you want roughly 1/125 s. This was shot at **1/15 s** - about three stops slower. So either it was on a tripod, or there is motion blur in here somewhere. Zoom in later and decide for yourself.
%[text] - f/10 is a fairly small aperture, which buys depth of field but costs light - and that is what forced the slow shutter and ISO 500. Change any one corner of the triangle and the other two have to move. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DA43C3D7] ## Open the CFA - the mosaiced image
%[text] **CFA** stands for color filter array. This is what the image sensor captures. The function `rawread` reads in the CFA (no special course function needed).
cfa = rawread('vertebra.dng'); % the whole Tiff/SubIFD block, in one line
%[text] And `rawinfo` hands you the three facts we worked hardest for:
rawInfo.CFALayout % the Bayer pattern - no need to decode tag 33422
rawInfo.ColorInfo.BlackLevel
rawInfo.ColorInfo.WhiteLevel
%[text] - the height and width of CFA are slightly different than RGB version
%[text] - cfa is a grayscale image (not an RGB image) because the color values have not be interpolated yet \
%%
%[text] ### **What are the bit depth and usable code range of the CFA?**
min(cfa,[],"all") % minimum intensity found
max(cfa,[],"all") % max intensity found
2^14 % the max value is close to this, so the sensor is 14-bit
%[text] This (roughly) matches the metadata:
meta.SubIFDs{1}.BlackLevel
meta.SubIFDs{1}.WhiteLevel
%[text] So the sensor records from a black level of 512 up to a white level of 16300. How much range is that, in the units photographers actually use?
fprintf('usable dynamic range: %.1f stops\n', log2(double(16300-512)));
fprintf('an 8-bit JPEG gives : 8.0 stops\n');
%[text] The white level is close to  `2^14`  suggesting that the sensor data use roughly 14 bits of numerical range even though MATLAB stores the values in a `uint16` array.  This is why this file is 51 MB instead of 5 MB. The DNG contains the full-resolution sensor data plus metadata and one or more rendered previews. The RAW sensor data account for most of the file size.
%%
%[text] ### Was this shot well exposed?
%[text] Highlights are "clipped" when pixels hit the white level and stop recording - that detail is gone for good and no amount of editing brings it back.
s = sprintf('pixels at or above the white level: %.4f%%\n', 100*mean(cfa>=16300,'all'));
s = [s sprintf('pixels below the black level      : %.2f%%\n', 100*mean(cfa<512,'all'))];
fprintf('%sbrightest pixel recorded          : %d  (ceiling is 16300)\n', s,max(cfa,[],'all'));
%[text] Two things fall out of this:
%[text] - **Nothing is clipped**, and the brightest pixel only reaches about 14,400 of a possible 16,300. There was close to a stop of headroom left unused. This shot is slightly **underexposed** - which is exactly why it looked so dark a moment ago, and why normalizing it matters so much. Photographers call the fix "expose to the right": push the histogram as far right as you can without clipping, because the upper stops hold the most tonal information.
%[text] - A small number of pixels read *below* the black level. That is **read noise** - the sensor's own electrical noise dipping under the calibrated zero point. It is a useful reminder that the black level is a calibration constant, not a measurement. \
%%
%[text] ### View CFA
%[text] We can view the CFA using imageViewer
imageViewer(cfa);
%[text] - Click on the contrast tool to improve contrast
%[text] - Zoom in to see the Bayer Filter pattern
%[text] - Try changing the color map \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C7D40956] ## Demosaic the CFA
%[text] Let's create the RGB image from the CFA image. To do that you have to know the camera's Bayer pattern, which is listed in the CFALayout field of `rawInfo`
rawInfo.CFALayout
%[text] You can then use the function **demosaic** to demosaic the RGB and create an RGB image with three channels
% bayer_pattern = 'rggb';
bayer_pattern = rawInfo.CFALayout;
RGB = demosaic(cfa,bayer_pattern); % The imaging toolbox includes a demosaic function
figure;
nexttile
imshow(RGB);
nexttile
mmHistColor(RGB)
%[text] - Now we have an RGB image
%[text] - Notice that `demosaic` returns an image the *same* height and width as the CFA, just with a third dimension added for the color planes.
%[text] - So why was the `rgb` image at the top of the script only 4000x6000? Because the camera trims the sensor border when it makes its own conversion, and the DNG says so explicitly: look at `DefaultCropOrigin` = \[12,12\] and `DefaultCropSize` = \[6000,4000\] in the SubIFD metadata. Twelve pixels are shaved off each edge. Sensors have a margin of pixels around the edge that are not trustworthy - some are masked off entirely and used to measure the black level. \
%[text] Why do you think the results are so dark and hard to see?
%[text] - The sensor is 14-bit, so its values top out around 16,384. But the data is stored in a 16-bit container, and `imshow` scales it as though the full 16-bit range were in use. Everything we captured is crammed into the bottom quarter of the display range. We will fix this when we normalize.  \
%%
%[text] Let's see if we can figure out what the demosaicing function did.
idx = 1:2 % look at the top-left 4x4 corner of the image
cfa(idx, idx)
%[text] Remember, the Bayer pattern repeats this 2x2 block:
%[text] `R G`
%[text] `G B`
%[text] So, these numbers represent those color intensities
RGB(idx, idx,:)
%[text] Notice that the original CFA values reappear, unchanged, in the corresponding planes of the demosaiced image: the red plane keeps the true red readings, the green plane keeps the true green readings, and so on. Every *other* number was **interpolated** - estimated from the neighboring pixels that did measure that color. That is what demosaicing is: two thirds of every RGB image from a Bayer sensor is an educated guess.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4AD7912E] ## Normalize the Image
%[text] To improve the ability to see the image, we will use **`mat2gray`**  to normalize RGB. However, instead of normalizing the maximum pixel intensities found in the image, we will normalize by the Black and  White levels indicated in the rawInfo metadata (so, we are including additional inputs into **`mat2gray`**).
BlackLevel = double(rawInfo.ColorInfo.BlackLevel(1))

WhiteLevel = double(rawInfo.ColorInfo.WhiteLevel(1))
%[text] Here we normalize the image so 512 becomes zero and 16300 becomes 1 
RGBn = im2uint16(mat2gray(RGB,[BlackLevel WhiteLevel])); % normalize then convert back to integer class for memory purposes

figure
nexttile
imshow(RGBn)
title('Demosaiced Image')
nexttile
mmHistColor(RGBn)
%[text] Hmm - now it has a greenish cast. Why do you think that is?
mean(RGBn,[1 2]) % the mean level of each channel, R G B
%[text] So why is the image so green? Notice that the Bayer pattern contains twice as many green samples as red or blue samples. This improves spatial sampling of luminance detail, but can add a greenish cast to the unprocessed image. Also, the three sensor channels do not respond equally to the same illumination. Cameras compensate for those differences using white-balance gains. Before those gains are applied, RAW sensor data often have a strong color cast.  So raw sensor data is *supposed* to look green. This is not a defect - it is what unprocessed means. Correcting it is the job of white balance, which is why white balance is a required step rather than an artistic choice. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_278B416B] ## Contrast stretch with imadjust
p.rgb = rgb; % white balanced on the camera 

IMG_lims = stretchlim(RGBn); % returns values that can be plugged into imadjust
p.stretched_IMG = imadjust(RGBn,IMG_lims,[0.01 0.99]); % 2nd input = input limits, 3rd = OUTPUT limits
figure % start a new figure - otherwise these tiles land in the previous one
nexttile
imshow(p.stretched_IMG)
title('Truecolor Composite after Contrast Stretch')
nexttile
mmHistColor(p.stretched_IMG)
%[text] That's better. However, there is still a slight yellowish cast. Lets try a better white balance adjustment 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1D493D3F] ## White Balance
%[text] Let's see if we can improve the white balance. 
%[text] Use the function **`illumwhite`** to estimate the white balance correction. It assumes that the brightest 1% values across all three channels represent something white.
illuminant = illumwhite(RGBn); 
p.caIMG = chromadapt(RGBn, illuminant,"ColorSpace","linear-rgb");
p.caIMG = imadjust(p.caIMG, stretchlim(p.caIMG),[0.01 0.99]);
%%
%[text] Next, compare the results 
figure;
tiledlayout(3,2,"TileSpacing","compact");

ttls = {'rgb','Contrast Stretch' 'WB and Stretch'};
fns = fieldnames(p);
for n=1:numel(fns)

nexttile; 
imshow(p.(fns{n}));
title(ttls{n});

nexttile;
mmHistColor(p.(fns{n}));

end
%[text] The white balance is much improved with a couple of simple manipulations - but look closely at the middle row and you may still see a cast. Let's find out why. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_WBCOMPARE] ## How good was our white balance guess?
%[text] `illumwhite` **estimates** the illuminant by assuming the brightest pixels are white. But this is a raw file: the camera stored the white-balance setting used when the photograph was taken. That gives us a useful comparison with our image-based estimate. Neither one is guaranteed to be objectively correct, which is why a known neutral object in the scene is so valuable.
camWB = rawInfo.ColorInfo.CameraAsTakenWhiteBalance % the camera's own R G G B multipliers
%[text] To compare fairly, express both as multipliers relative to green (green is the reference channel, so it gets a multiplier of 1).
ourMult = max(double(illuminant))./double(illuminant); % turn our estimate into multipliers
ourMult = ourMult./ourMult(2); % normalize to green
camMult = [camWB(1) camWB(2) camWB(4)]./camWB(2); % camera version, dropping the duplicate green

fprintf('our illumwhite estimate: R %.2f  G %.2f  B %.2f\n', ourMult);
fprintf('the camera''s own choice: R %.2f  G %.2f  B %.2f\n', camMult);
%[text] 
%[text] Now the leftover cast makes sense. `illumwhite` under-boosts red by nearly a full stop and roughly *doubles* the blue the camera asked for. The "brightest pixels are white" assumption is a reasonable guess, but in this scene the brightest pixels were not actually neutral - the Exif even told us the light source was **Shade**, which is strongly blue, so a correct correction should be warming the image up, not cooling it further.
%[text] Let's apply the camera's numbers instead and compare all three.
%%
wb = im2double(RGBn); % work in double so scaling cannot clip
wb(:,:,1) = wb(:,:,1)*camMult(1); % boost red   (green multiplier is 1, so green is untouched)
wb(:,:,3) = wb(:,:,3)*camMult(3); % boost blue
wb = wb./max(wb,[],'all'); % bring the result back into the 0-1 range

p.wbRGB = im2uint16(wb);
p.wbRGB = imadjust(p.wbRGB, stretchlim(p.wbRGB),[0.01 0.99]); % same contrast stretch as the others
ttls{end+1} = 'Camera WB'
%%
%[text] MATLAB also includes the function **`raw2rgb`**, which does demosaic, white balance and color space conversion in a single call:
p.oneStep = raw2rgb("vertebra.dng");
ttls{end+1} = 'One Step'
%%
%[text] ### Compare all the white balance steps
figure
tiledlayout("flow","TileSpacing","tight","Padding","tight")
% mmTightTiledLayout
fns = fieldnames(p);
for n=1:numel(fns)
    nexttile
    imshow(p.(fns{n}))
    title(ttls{n})
end
%[text] **Use the coin as reference.** There is a quarter lying next to the vertebra, and a coin is very close to a neutral gray - which makes it an accidental **gray card**. This is exactly how photographers check white balance in the field: find something you know is neutral and ask whether it renders neutral.
%[text] - In the no-white-balance version the coin is distinctly warm.
%[text] - In the `illumwhite` version it has gone cool and slightly purple - over-corrected.
%[text] - In the camera version it reads as plain silver-gray, and the bone finally looks like bone.
%[text] - This is the real lesson about white balance: there is no single "correct" answer that an algorithm can always recover from pixels alone. The camera knew things the algorithm did not - the metering mode, the light source, and what the photographer told it. That information travels with the raw file, and it is thrown away the moment you export a JPEG. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B20952E0] ## Raw vs RGB
%[text] Let's zoom in on some pixels to compare the difference
%[text] - Run the code
%[text] - Select a region to compare
%[text] - Then double-click \
figure;
[crop_IMG,rect] = imcrop(stretched_IMG);
crop_cfa = imcrop(cfa,rect);
montage({imadjust(crop_cfa), crop_IMG})
title('RAW | White balanced')
%[text] At first glance the RAW looks sharper - but be careful here, because two different things are going on.
%[text] - **The artifact:** what you are looking at on the left is the *undemosaiced* Bayer mosaic, shown as grayscale. Neighboring pixels sit behind different colored filters, so the mosaic itself adds a fine checkerboard at the pixel scale. Your eye reads that as crispness, but it is not resolved detail.
%[text] - **The real effect:** demosaicing genuinely does cost resolution and genuinely does introduce edge artifacts - look for the color fringes and the slight roughness along high-contrast edges on the right. Two thirds of those pixels were interpolated, so fine detail gets smeared. \
%[text] So the honest summary is not "raw is sharper". It is that demosaicing is a reconstruction, and reconstructions lose something. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_7CD0912A] ## Take Home Points
%[text] - Raw images are the digital equivalent of a film negative: a raw representation of the original, captured image.
%[text] - Raw images are large and difficult to work with. However, they offer greater control on how the image will look at the end.
%[text] - Demosaicing is a **reconstruction**: only one third of the color values in a Bayer image were ever measured, and the rest are interpolated. That costs a little resolution and introduces edge artifacts, though usually not at a scale you would notice in a print.
%[text] - A raw file holds roughly **14 stops** of tonal range against about 8 for an 8-bit JPEG. That is what you are paying for with the file size, and it is what gives you room to recover shadows and highlights later.
%[text] - A raw file also carries the **entire record of the exposure** - shutter, aperture, ISO, focal length, metering mode, light source, and the camera's own white balance. Exporting a JPEG throws most of that away.
%[text] - There is no purely algorithmic "correct" white balance. `illumwhite` guesses from the pixels; the camera knew the scene. Find something neutral in the frame and judge against it. \

